  card="Biodiversity is the variety of life on Earth, including plants, animals, microorganisms and the ecosystems they inhabit. It refers not only to the number of different species but also to the genetic differences within species and the variety of habitats and ecological processes that sustain life: the richness of life forms and the natural balance they create, essential for healthy ecosystems and human survival. Since biodiversity is unevenly distributed across the Earth, geography plays a vital role in conservation. Key aspects are: hotspots of biodiversity such as the Himalaya, the Western Ghats, North-East India and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, rich in unique species found nowhere else; ecosystem-based conservation suited to the geographical setting, such as mangrove protection on coasts or afforestation in mountains; protected areas such as national parks, wildlife sanctuaries, biosphere reserves and World Heritage Sites to preserve critical habitats and prevent fragmentation; geographical barriers and corridors, like rivers and mountains, which influence animal movement and gene flow so wildlife corridors can be planned; and human-environment interaction through sustainable practices such as terrace farming or agroforestry.",
